मिट्टी, घास और जंगल बारिश को स्पंज की तरह पी जाते हैं। सड़कें, छतें और पार्किंग नहीं पीतीं। शहर में लगभग सारी बारिश सख़्त सतहों से सीधे नालों में बहती है, जो जल्दी भर जाते हैं। जब नाले [[@plastic-made|प्लास्टिक]] और कचरे से बंद हो जाते हैं, तो पानी के पास जाने की कोई जगह नहीं बचती।

दलदल और कच्छ उलटे ढंग से मदद करते हैं। [[@wetlands|आर्द्रभूमियाँ]] बाढ़ का पानी सोखकर उसे धीरे-धीरे छोड़ती हैं, इसीलिए बहुत-से शहर अब अपनी बची-खुची झीलों और दलदलों को बचाने की कोशिश कर रहे हैं।

{{story:wetlands}}

In 1782, two French brothers who ran a paper factory, Joseph-Michel and Jacques-Étienne Montgolfier, noticed that hot smoke made paper bags rise. They built bigger and bigger bags of cloth and paper.

On 19 September 1783, at the palace of Versailles, they sent up a balloon carrying a sheep, a duck and a rooster, watched by King Louis XVI. The animals landed safely after about eight minutes. Two months later, on 21 November, two men floated across [[@paris|Paris]] for about 25 minutes. It was the first time people had flown freely.

{{story:fly-without-wings}}

For thousands of years, kings collected strange animals to impress visitors. The oldest zoo still open today, at Schönbrunn Palace in Vienna, began as an emperor's collection in 1752. [[@kolkata|Kolkata]]'s Alipore Zoo opened in 1876. For a long time it was home to Adwaita, [[@turtles-tortoises|a giant tortoise]] said to be well over 150 years old when he died in 2006.

Older zoos kept animals in small cages with bars. Many modern ones use larger spaces with plants, water and places to hide. Keepers give animals puzzles, hidden food and new smells to [[@boredom|stop boredom]]. This is called enrichment. A bear might have to work out how to open a log stuffed with honey, the way it would rip into a bees' nest in the wild. In India, every zoo must be approved by the Central Zoo Authority, set up in 1992, which sets rules for space and care.

{{story:zoos-history}}

On a bicycle, a chain links a big gear at the pedals to smaller gears at the back wheel. In a low gear, the chain sits on a large back gear. Each push of the pedal turns the wheel only a little, so climbing a hill feels easier, though you go slowly. In a high gear, the chain moves to a small back gear. The wheel spins quickly with every pedal stroke, which is great on a flat road but hard work uphill.

Racing cyclists may have more than 20 gear combinations to choose from. Car gearboxes do the same job for an engine, which works best at certain speeds. Low gears get a heavy car moving. High gears let it cruise without the engine racing.

{{story:antikythera}}

Forgers try to copy money, so banknotes are packed with tricks. Intaglio printing presses ink so hard that it stands up from the paper. Colour-shifting ink changes from green to blue as you tilt the note. Micro-lettering is so small it looks like a line until you use a magnifying glass.

Some features glow under [[ultraviolet|ultraviolet]] light. Others are [[@holograms|holograms]] or shiny strips. Indian notes have a see-through register, where parts of a design on the front and back fit together perfectly when held up to light. They also carry raised marks so that people who are blind can tell values apart.

{{story:made-coins}}

पश्चिम अफ़्रीका में, ख़ासकर [[@nigeria|नाइजीरिया के योरूबा लोगों]] में, बोलने वाला ढोल रेत-घड़ी के आकार का होता है, और उसकी दोनों खालों के बीच डोरियाँ बँधी होती हैं। बजाने वाला उसे एक बगल में दबाकर निचोड़ता है। डोरियाँ जितनी कसीं, सुर उतना ऊँचा, कुछ-कुछ हमारे डमरू की याद दिलाता हुआ।

योरूबा सुर वाली भाषा है: एक ही आवाज़ ऊँचे या नीचे सुर में बोलने पर अलग मतलब देती है। शब्दों के सुरों की नकल करके हुनरमंद ढोलवादक नाम, [[@life-greetings|अभिवादन]] और प्रशंसा की कविताएँ "बोल" सकता है, जिन्हें सुनने वाले समझ लेते हैं। यह [[@codes-morse|मोर्स कोड]] से अलग है, जो हर अक्षर को बिंदुओं और डैश में बदलता है।

{{story:codes-morse}}

पारंपरिक चित्रकार अपने बहुत-से रंग खुद बनाते हैं। सफ़ेद शंख के चूर्ण से आता है। काला दीये से जमा की गई कालिख से। लाल और पीला पिसे हुए [[mineral|खनिजों]] से, और बाकी रंग पौधों से। इन चूर्णों को कैथ के पेड़ के गोंद में मिलाया जाता है, ताकि ये चिपके रहें।

ब्रश भी घर पर बनते हैं। बारीक ब्रश चूहे या गिलहरी के कुछ बालों को एक डंडी पर बाँधकर बनाए जाते थे, और मोटे ब्रश भैंस के बालों से। चित्रकार पहले हाशिया बनाता है, फिर आकृतियों की बाहरी रेखाएँ खींचता है, उनमें सपाट रंग भरता है, और आखिर में नाज़ुक डिज़ाइन जोड़ता है।

{{story:storytelling-india}}

People have argued about taxes for thousands of years, and some arguments became revolutions. In 1773, American colonists angry at a British tax on tea threw crates of it into [[@boston|Boston Harbour]]. Their slogan was "no taxation without representation": they would not pay taxes set by a [[parliament|parliament]] they could not vote for. Within a few years the [[@american-revolution|American Revolution]] had begun.

In 1930, [[@gandhi|Mahatma Gandhi]] led thousands of people on a walk of about 385 km to the sea at Dandi to make salt, breaking a British law that taxed it. Salt was something even the poorest family needed, so the tax touched everyone.

{{story:salt-march}}

In 1971, the cartoonist Pran Kumar Sharma, known as Pran, created Chacha Chaudhary. He is a small old man with a white moustache, a red turban and a walking stick. He outwits robbers and fixes problems with common sense.

His friend Sabu is a giant from the planet [[@jupiter-giant|Jupiter]]. When Sabu gets angry, a volcano erupts somewhere on Jupiter, or so the comics say. Chacha Chaudhary appeared in Hindi and many other languages, and later became a TV series.

{{story:panchatantra-tales}}

कुछ सफ़ेद रक्त कोशिकाएँ, जिन्हें फ़ैगोसाइट कहते हैं, कीटाणु के चारों ओर लिपटकर उसे साबुत निगल जाती हैं और फिर पचा डालती हैं। इन्हें 1882 में रूसी वैज्ञानिक इल्या मेचनिकोव ने खोजा, जो इटली के मेसिना शहर में काम कर रहे थे। उन्होंने एक पारदर्शी [[@starfish|तारामछली]] के [[larva|लार्वा]] में गुलाब का काँटा चुभोया। अगली सुबह उन्होंने देखा कि काँटे के चारों ओर कोशिकाओं की भीड़ लगी है, जो उस पर हमला कर रही हैं। इस खोज के लिए 1908 में उन्हें नोबेल पुरस्कार में हिस्सा मिला।

जब ये कोशिकाएँ लड़ती हैं, तो वह जगह अक्सर लाल, गर्म और सूजी हुई हो जाती है। इसे सूजन या इन्फ़्लेमेशन कहते हैं। ज़्यादा खून वहाँ ज़्यादा सिपाही पहुँचाता है।

{{story:microscopes}}

The robot vacuum called Roomba went on sale in the US in 2002. Early models were fairly simple. They bumped into something, turned a little and tried again, and their wandering path slowly covered most of a room.

Newer ones are cleverer. Many spin a [[@lasers|laser]] on top to measure distances to walls, or use a camera pointed at the ceiling. From those readings they draw a map, then clean in neat rows like a farmer ploughing a field. Underneath, "cliff [[sensor|sensors]]" fire [[infrared|infrared]] light at the floor so the robot stops at the top of the stairs.

{{story:vacuum-cleaners}}

Living things take in a tiny amount of a special kind of carbon. When they die, it slowly turns into something else at a steady rate, losing half every 5,730 years. Measuring what's left in a bone, seed or piece of charcoal tells scientists roughly when it died. This is radiocarbon dating, invented by the American chemist Willard Libby in the late 1940s. It works for things up to about 50,000 years old. The steady rate of loss is called a [[halflife|half-life]].

{{story:half-life}}

Tree rings help too. Each year a tree adds a ring, wide in good years and thin in bad ones. Matching patterns between old timbers can date a wooden beam to the exact year it was cut.

For about six years, Siddhartha studied with teachers and tried extreme hardship, eating almost nothing. Stories say he grew so thin you could see his spine through his belly. It did not bring wisdom.

So he ate a meal and chose what he called the Middle Way, between luxury and harsh self-punishment. He sat under a pipal tree, a kind of fig like the [[@banyan|banyan]], and meditated. Buddhists believe he reached enlightenment there at about 35. A [[descendant|descendant]] of that tree still grows beside the Mahabodhi Temple.

{{story:jataka-tales}}

Iltutmish chose his daughter Razia to rule after him, saying she was more able than his sons. In 1236 she became sultan, the only woman to rule from Delhi's throne in this era.

Razia dressed in a cloak and turban, held court in public and rode an elephant at the head of her army. Many nobles disliked taking orders from a woman. After about four years she was overthrown and killed. Her story has inspired books, plays and a film.

{{story:urdu}}

The Lumière brothers ran a big photography factory in Lyon. Their machine, the Cinématographe, weighed only about 5 kg. It could film, print and [[@projectors|project]] all in one box, turned by a hand crank.

Films ran at about 16 pictures a second. Your eye and brain blend the still frames into smooth motion, which is the trick behind [[@movies-move|why movies seem to move]]. Their films had no sound and no colour, and almost none of them told a story. People came to see ordinary life on a giant screen: waves on a beach, a street, a boat leaving harbour.

{{story:photography-history}}

At the National Air and Space Museum, you can stand under the [[@wright-brothers|Wright brothers']] 1903 Flyer, the first successful powered aeroplane. Nearby is the capsule that brought the Apollo 11 astronauts home from the Moon. A sister museum in Virginia holds the space shuttle Discovery.

The National Museum of Natural History has a huge Tyrannosaurus rex skeleton, an elephant in its main hall and the Hope Diamond, a deep blue stone of about 45 carats. For anyone who loves [[@gems-crystals|gems and crystals]], the mineral hall nearby can easily take an afternoon.

{{story:wright-brothers}}

Most people in Tokyo travel by train. Lines run above and below ground, operated by many different companies. Guinness World Records lists Shinjuku Station as the [[@extreme-busiest-places|busiest station in the world]], used by around 3 million passengers a day.

Trains are so frequent on some lines that they come every two or three minutes. At rush hour, they get extremely crowded, and station staff sometimes help push people in so the doors can close. In 1964, Japan opened the first [[@bullet-trains|bullet train]], the [[@maglev|Shinkansen]], linking Tokyo and Osaka just before the [[@olympics-host|Tokyo Olympics]].

{{story:bullet-trains}}

Zamenhof grew up in Białystok, a town that was then in the Russian Empire and is now in Poland. On its streets he heard Russian, Polish, German and Yiddish. The different groups often distrusted each other, and he came to believe that not understanding one another's languages made things worse.

As a teenager he began building a language. He chose words mostly from European languages, especially Latin, French and German, so that many people would recognise them. Then he made the grammar as simple as possible, with no exceptions.

{{story:translation-apps}}

In the 1700s, British inventors built spinning machines, and factories in Lancashire began making cotton cloth by the mile. This was a key part of the [[@industrial-revolution|Industrial Revolution]]. Cheap factory cloth flooded India and hurt millions of hand-spinners and weavers.

{{story:industrial-revolution}}

In the United States, Eli Whitney's cotton gin of 1794 cleaned cotton far faster. Cotton farming spread across the South, and so did [[@us-civil-war|slavery]], because plantation owners forced enslaved people to grow and pick it. The story of American cotton is tied to the story of [[@slavery-freedom|slavery and freedom]].

When betel leaf, kattha and lime are chewed together, they react and turn the [[saliva|saliva]] bright red. That is why paan chewers have red lips and why the streets of some cities are dotted with red splashes.

Those splashes cause real damage. In 2013, engineers in Kolkata found that spat paan was eating away at the steel casings at the base of [[@howrah-bridge|Howrah Bridge]]'s pillars. The mix of saliva and lime is so corrosive that the bridge's protective covers had worn much thinner. The fix was to wrap them in fibreglass and ask people to stop spitting.

{{story:howrah-bridge}}

A watermelon vine grows two kinds of yellow flowers. Male flowers make [[pollen|pollen]]. Female flowers have a tiny striped bump behind the petals, a baby watermelon in waiting. A bee has to carry pollen from one to the other, often visiting the same flower several times.

If too little pollen arrives, the fruit grows lopsided or drops off. That's why many farmers in the USA rent beehives when their melons flower. Without [[@pollinators|bees and other pollinators]], there would be very few watermelons.

{{story:pollinators}}
